  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The paper is a theoretical commentary and does not present empirical data or specific case studies to test the proposed framework.",
      "what": "This commentary presents a conceptual framework analyzing how contemporary nationalist projects, driven by democratic backsliding and populist movements over the last two decades, are enacted and contested within urban environments. It conceptualizes cities not just as nodes of global capital, but as active infrastructures of nationalist governance and sites of grassroots political resistance where citizenship and belonging are reconfigured.",
      "why": "It challenges the state-centric bias in nationalism studies and the denationalized view of cities in global urban studies, establishing a theoretical bridge to analyze how national political agendas are physically negotiated through everyday municipal practices."
